# PDS Meridian

PDS Meridian is the policy-driven grading and reporting layer for Paper Data Suite.

Meridian is currently in its initial architecture and documentation phase. No production grading, reporting, storage, or delivery implementation exists yet.

## Purpose

Meridian will transform authorized academic evidence into explainable standards proficiency, Grades, and reports under explicit, versioned policies.

Its responsibilities will include:

* standards-based proficiency calculation;
* Grade-item membership;
* evidence and attempt selection;
* reassessment handling;
* conventional, standards-based, and hybrid Grade policies;
* Academic Period aggregation;
* teacher overrides;
* report composition;
* report snapshots and provenance;
* report subscriptions;
* and coordination with authorized report-delivery systems.

Meridian will consume Core-governed academic work registrations and Publication Records while preserving the meaning and authority of producer-owned records.

## Architectural Role

Paper Data Suite separates canonical infrastructure, producer-native records, grading policy, and reporting policy across distinct modules.

### PDS Core

PDS Core owns shared canonical infrastructure, including:

* Academic Period calendars;
* academic work registrations;
* immutable Publication Records;
* publication series and withdrawals;
* manifest paths and digests;
* shared routing identities;
* producer compatibility metadata;
* and the disposable academic registry catalog.

Core determines whether shared records are structurally valid and how they are located.

Core does not calculate:

* standards proficiency;
* assignment Grades;
* reporting-period Grades;
* cumulative course Grades;
* evidence-selection results;
* or final cross-module reports.

### Producer Modules

Producer modules own their native records and domain-specific interpretation.

Examples include:

* ScoreForm;
* Quillan;
* Concord;
* Portia;
* and future Paper Data Suite producers.

Producer modules publish faithful results through Core-governed registry contracts.

They may provide information such as:

* scores;
* rubric results;
* criterion ratings;
* standards-aligned evidence;
* written feedback;
* intervention records;
* source-record references;
* and producer-specific capabilities.

Producer modules do not decide authoritative cumulative proficiency or course Grades for Meridian.

### Meridian

Meridian will:

* select eligible academic evidence;
* apply explicit grading policy;
* calculate standards proficiency;
* determine Grade-item results;
* derive conventional Grades when required;
* aggregate results across Academic Periods;
* preserve teacher overrides;
* compose authorized reports;
* create reproducible report snapshots;
* and explain how each derived result was produced.

Meridian will not replace producer-owned records or Core’s canonical registry.

## Standards-Based Grading

Standards-based grading is a first-class Meridian capability.

It will not be implemented merely as a presentation layer over a conventional points ledger.

Meridian must eventually support:

* academic work associated with one or more focus standards;
* repeated observations of the same standard;
* evidence from multiple authorized producers;
* configurable proficiency scales;
* four-level proficiency scales;
* configurable evidence-selection strategies;
* reassessment;
* recency;
* teacher-selected evidence;
* teacher overrides;
* insufficient-evidence states;
* reporting-period proficiency;
* cumulative proficiency;
* and optional conversion to conventional Grades.

Possible evidence-selection policies may include:

* most recent evidence;
* highest evidence;
* decaying averages;
* weighted evidence;
* bounded evidence windows;
* teacher-selected evidence;
* or other explicitly configured strategies.

Meridian will not prescribe one universal grading formula.

Courses, schools, and districts may require different:

* proficiency scales;
* level labels;
* evidence-selection policies;
* minimum-evidence rules;
* weighting policies;
* rounding rules;
* and Grade-conversion policies.

Those decisions must be explicit, versioned, and explainable.

## Distinct Academic Concepts

Meridian must preserve distinctions among:

* producer-native results;
* assignment or work performance;
* standards evidence;
* calculated standards proficiency;
* Grade-item results;
* reporting-period Grades;
* cumulative course Grades;
* and report presentation.

These concepts must not be collapsed into one generic score.

Meridian must also distinguish non-Grade states such as:

* insufficient evidence;
* not yet assessed;
* missing;
* incomplete;
* excused;
* excluded;
* withdrawn;
* and invalid.

These states must not automatically become zero.

## Grade-Item Membership

A producer publication does not automatically become a Grade item.

Meridian must apply explicit policy to determine whether registered work contributes to:

* standards proficiency;
* a Grade-item result;
* a reporting-period Grade;
* a cumulative Grade;
* or no Grade calculation at all.

Grade-item membership must not be inferred solely from:

* the existence of a publication;
* the publication kind;
* the producer module;
* the presence of a manifest;
* or a numeric value in a producer-native record.

## Academic Periods

Meridian will use Core-owned Academic Period definitions.

Core remains authoritative for:

* school-year calendars;
* marking periods;
* semesters;
* quarters;
* and other canonical Academic Period relationships.

Meridian owns the policy that determines how eligible evidence and Grade items contribute to calculations associated with those periods.

Meridian does not redefine the canonical Academic Period calendar.

## Conventional and Hybrid Grading

Meridian must support multiple grading models.

### Pure standards-based reporting

The primary outputs are proficiency levels by standard.

A conventional percentage or letter Grade may not be required.

### Conventional grading

Where required, Meridian may calculate:

* points;
* percentages;
* weighted categories;
* or other institution-approved conventional Grades.

### Hybrid grading

Meridian may derive a conventional Grade from standards proficiency under an explicit conversion policy.

Any conversion must be:

* configured;
* versioned;
* reproducible;
* institution-specific;
* and visible in calculation provenance.

Meridian must not silently convert standards evidence into a conventional Grade.

## Academic and Intervention Information

Academic-result publications and intervention publications are semantically distinct.

Portia intervention records may be included in authorized:

* support reports;
* progress reports;
* intervention summaries;
* instructional dashboards;
* or multidisciplinary review materials.

Their inclusion in a report does not automatically make them:

* Grade items;
* standards evidence;
* assessment attempts;
* proficiency observations;
* or conventional Grade components.

Meridian may present academic and intervention information together while preserving their separate meanings and authority.

## Explainability and Provenance

Meridian calculations must remain explainable.

Every derived proficiency, Grade, or report snapshot should eventually identify:

* source Publication Record IDs;
* source registration revisions;
* selected evidence;
* excluded evidence;
* applicable standards;
* applicable Academic Periods;
* grading-policy version;
* report-definition version;
* authorized overrides;
* calculation time;
* and supersession or refresh state.

Given the same:

* canonical source records;
* grading policy;
* evidence-selection policy;
* Academic Period definitions;
* and overrides,

Meridian should produce the same derived result.

Recalculation under a new policy must not erase the provenance of an earlier calculation or issued report.

## Teacher Overrides

Meridian may support authorized teacher overrides.

An override must preserve:

* the underlying evidence;
* the originally calculated value;
* the replacement value;
* the responsible actor;
* the time of the override;
* and any required rationale.

An override must not silently rewrite producer-owned evidence.

## Reports

Meridian reports will be derived products.

They will not replace or mutate the Core registrations and Publication Records from which they were produced.

A report definition may eventually specify:

* report type;
* intended audience;
* student or cohort scope;
* Academic Period scope;
* included academic evidence;
* included intervention information;
* grading policy;
* reporting policy;
* authorization requirements;
* and presentation requirements.

## Report Snapshots

Issued reports should be reproducible snapshots rather than untraceable live views.

A report snapshot should eventually identify:

* source Publication Records;
* relevant registration revisions;
* Academic Period calendar revision;
* grading-policy version;
* report-definition version;
* generated time;
* intended audience;
* overrides;
* and source-selection decisions.

Meridian must distinguish:

* frozen report snapshots representing what was issued at a particular time;
* and refreshable views generated from current authorized state.

Refreshing a report must not silently rewrite the provenance of a previously issued snapshot.

## Report Subscriptions

A report subscription will represent a request for repeated or condition-based report generation.

Possible triggers may include:

* a schedule;
* an Academic Period milestone;
* publication availability;
* report-definition changes;
* teacher request;
* or another explicit event.

A subscription does not become an authoritative academic data source.

Meridian may coordinate delivery or pass generated reports to an authorized delivery component. Its calculation engine does not need to own every email, messaging, export, or presentation channel.

## Security and Data Minimization

Meridian is expected to process sensitive educational information.

Future implementations must account for:

* authorization;
* report audience selection;
* student-record access;
* source-publication access;
* Grade and proficiency integrity;
* override authorization;
* report delivery;
* report snapshot provenance;
* subscription triggers;
* exported files;
* and integration secrets.

Reports should include only the information authorized and necessary for their intended purpose.

See [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) for vulnerability-reporting guidance.

## Architectural Decisions

The initial Meridian architectural decisions are documented in:

* [ADR 0001: Policy-Driven Standards Proficiency and Grade Calculation](docs/decisions/0001-policy-driven-standards-proficiency-and-grade-calculation.md)
* [ADR 0002: Provenance-Bound Report Snapshots and Subscriptions](docs/decisions/0002-provenance-bound-report-snapshots-and-subscriptions.md)

Accepted ADRs record architectural decisions rather than implementation completeness.

Material reversals should be documented through a superseding ADR rather than silently rewriting an accepted decision.

## Current Status

This repository currently contains architectural and repository documentation only.

It does not yet contain:

* a Meridian application;
* a Python package;
* runtime APIs;
* database schemas;
* report schemas;
* grading-policy schemas;
* calculation engines;
* subscriptions;
* delivery integrations;
* or production-ready functionality.

The documentation establishes ownership and boundaries before implementation begins.

## Out of Scope

Meridian does not own:

* raw scan ingestion;
* OCR;
* rubric-authoring interfaces;
* producer-native record schemas;
* canonical registry writes;
* Core’s Academic Period model;
* Core’s publication registry;
* producer-specific manifest parsing;
* intervention case management;
* roster authority;
* identity authority;
* arbitrary modification of producer records;
* or Core’s disposable registry catalog.

Meridian also must not silently reinterpret arbitrary producer files or flatten all producer outputs into one universal numeric score.

